(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["pharmacology-i-exam-2-pharm-exam-2-master-exam-form-e"]={"title":"Pharmacology I Exam 2 Master Exam — Form E","category":"Pharmacology I","exam":"Exam 2","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"Why is ibuprofen a problem for a patient taking methotrexate?","choices":["It displaces methotrexate from its receptor","It prevents methotrexate from being absorbed","It inactivates methotrexate in the liver","It reduces methotrexate secretion"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Same mechanism as the lithium interaction -- reduced renal clearance of a drug that does harm when it accumulates. Producing toxic levels. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"A 66-year-old man has chest pressure at rest and an electrocardiogram suggesting an acute coronary syndrome. He had an intracranial hemorrhage last week. Blood pressure is 130/80 mmHg and heart rate is 80 beats per minute. Which of the following is contraindicated?","choices":["Aspirin","Nitroglycerin","Metoprolol","Morphine"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Recent intracranial hemorrhage is a listed contraindication to aspirin, along with allergy and recent gastrointestinal bleeding. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 53"},{"q":"Which second messenger do nitrates increase to relax vascular smooth muscle?","choices":["Cyclic adenosine monophosphate","Cyclic guanosine monophosphate","Inositol trisphosphate","Cytosolic calcium"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Nitric oxide from nitrates stimulates conversion of guanosine triphosphate to cyclic guanosine monophosphate, which lowers calcium and relaxes smooth muscle. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"Which systemic adverse effects can a topical ophthalmic beta blocker produce?","choices":["Hypertension and tachycardia","Central nervous system depression and apnea","Bradycardia, heart block and bronchoconstriction","Bitter taste and allergic conjunctivitis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A drop is not a local-only treatment — nasolacrimal absorption avoids first-pass metabolism, so a topical beta blocker can do everything a systemic one does. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 67"},{"q":"Otic preparations usually combine an anti-infective with a glucocorticoid. What does each component do?","choices":["The steroid kills the organism and the anti-infective reduces swelling","Both components are antibacterial and act synergistically","The anti-infective inhibits bacterial growth and the steroid reduces inflammatory cytokines","The steroid improves penetration of the anti-infective through the tympanic membrane"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Two separate jobs in one bottle, which is also why the combinations cost more than a plain antibiotic drop. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"Clonidine has analgesic activity. What additional use does this support?","choices":["Migraine prophylaxis","Prostatic hyperplasia","Essential tremor","Blunting opiate withdrawal"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Clonidine's analgesic activity blunts opiate withdrawal reactions. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 93"},{"q":"Why is a patient who has taken a corticosteroid for more than about a week told not to stop abruptly?","choices":["The drug accumulates and must be cleared slowly","Stopping suddenly produces rebound symptoms","Abrupt withdrawal causes fluid retention","The drug loses effect if restarted after a gap"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. It is the duration that creates the problem, which is why a short course and a long one are stopped differently. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 46"},{"q":"How do ocular nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drugs reduce inflammation?","choices":["They inhibit phospholipase A2, preventing arachidonic acid formation","They stabilize mast cells against degranulation","They block cyclooxygenase","They inactivate the H1 receptor"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. They act one step below the steroids: the steroids stop arachidonic acid being liberated at all, while the nonsteroidals stop what is already liberated becoming prostaglandin. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"A patient has taken cholestyramine for years. Which deficiency is she at risk of?","choices":["Potassium, magnesium and zinc","Fat-soluble vitamins and folate","Water-soluble B vitamins and iron","Sodium, chloride and bicarbonate"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Resins cause malabsorption of vitamins A, D, E and K and of folic acid. — Lipids.pptx, Slide 46"},{"q":"A patient with hypertension also has heart failure. Which calcium channel blocker avoids the relative contraindication in heart failure?","choices":["Verapamil","Amlodipine","Diltiazem","Nimodipine"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Heart failure is a relative contraindication to the non-dihydropyridines because they suppress contractility; amlodipine, a dihydropyridine approved for hypertension, has little effect on contractility. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 46"},{"q":"Which pair of side effects is typical of central sympatholytics?","choices":["Cough and angioedema","Flushing and tachycardia","Hair growth and edema","Sedation and dry mouth"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Drowsiness or sedation and dry mouth are central effects; sexual dysfunction, a narrow therapeutic range and abrupt withdrawal hypertension complete the list. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 91"},{"q":"A 19-year-old contact lens wearer has purulent conjunctivitis. Keratitis has been excluded. Which class do you reach for, and why?","choices":["A macrolide, because it is soothing on the inflamed eye","A fluoroquinolone","An aminoglycoside, because it covers gram negatives","Sulfacetamide, because it is inexpensive"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and the same class is preferred for corneal ulcers for the same reason. Because lens wearers are at high risk for Pseudomonas. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"A 24-year-old college athlete has exertional chest pain and near-syncope. A systolic murmur grows louder with the Valsalva maneuver, and echocardiography shows asymmetric septal hypertrophy with outflow obstruction. Which drug class would be harmful?","choices":["A beta blocker","A non-dihydropyridine","A nitrate","An antiplatelet drug"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Obstructive cardiomyopathy is a listed nitrate contraindication, and aortic stenosis is the other structural one. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"Which adverse effect is specific enough to an ophthalmic antibiotic class to be worth knowing, rather than being shared by all of them?","choices":["Ocular irritation with erythromycin","Corneal ulceration from an aminoglycoside","Hypersensitivity with bacitracin","Unpleasant taste after a macrolide"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Ocular irritation is common to essentially every agent here and so distinguishes nothing — but aminoglycosides used for several days can produce corneal ulceration and a reactive keratoconjunctivitis, which changes how long you continue them. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"In which patient is a statin contraindicated?","choices":["A patient aged 50 with diabetes","A patient after a heart attack","A patient who is pregnant","A patient with LDL of 210 mg/dL"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Hepatic disease and pregnancy are the statin contraindications. — Lipids.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"Which classes INCREASE aqueous outflow?","choices":["Prostaglandins, alpha adrenergic agonists and cholinergic agonists","Beta blockers, carbonic anhydrase inhibitors and prostaglandins","Carbonic anhydrase inhibitors and cholinergic agonists only","Beta blockers alone"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The alpha agonists appear on both sides — they decrease production and increase outflow — which is why they are easy to misplace. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 64"},{"q":"Which lifestyle measure raises HDL (high-density lipoprotein) cholesterol?","choices":["Grapefruit juice","Exercise","Salt substitutes","Fasting before meals"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. HDL increases with exercise, a lifestyle measure that complements the drug classes that raise it, niacin and fibrates. — Lipids.pptx, Slide 62"},{"q":"How does the technique for a gel or ointment differ from a drop?","choices":["The lacrimal duct must be covered for twice as long","The eye should be held open rather than closed","It should be applied directly to the cornea rather than the pocket","A ribbon into the pocket, with no duct occlusion"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The process is otherwise the same, but ointment does not drain away like a solution, so the punctal occlusion step is unnecessary. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"How long may an over-the-counter ocular vasoconstrictor be used, and what should the patient be told if there is no improvement?","choices":["Under two weeks; see a provider if no better in 72 hours","Up to three months, with review if symptoms persist","Indefinitely, as they are available without prescription","Less than two weeks, but no review is needed if symptoms persist"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Short-term use only, because of rebound hyperemia. And the 72-hour rule exists because a red eye that is not improving may be something more serious than allergy. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 48"},{"q":"Which is an ABSOLUTE contraindication to niacin?","choices":["Peptic ulcer disease","History of symptomatic gout","Diabetes with glucose intolerance","Chronic liver disease"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Chronic liver disease is the absolute contraindication; peptic ulcer disease, symptomatic gout, significant hyperuricemia and diabetes are relative ones. — Lipids.pptx, Slide 56"},{"q":"For hypertension, which patient is a beta blocker expected to work best in?","choices":["A young patient with resting tachycardia","An older patient with asthma","A patient with intermittent claudication","A patient with frequent hypoglycemia"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. They work best in young patients with resting tachycardia, raised catecholamines and raised renin, though fatigue and limited exercise are common complaints. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 63"},{"q":"A patient on an inhaled steroid has oral thrush and takes several other medicines. Which antifungal is preferred, and why?","choices":["Ketoconazole, because it is the more potent agent","Either one, since neither is absorbed at all","Neither, since thrush resolves without treatment","Nystatin, because it is not absorbed"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The property that limits it to surface infection is the same one that keeps it out of the patient's other drugs. Not being absorbed is also why it cannot interact with the patient's other medicines. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"Which of these statins is one of the two that can reach high intensity?","choices":["Atorvastatin","Simvastatin","Pitavastatin","Pravastatin"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Atorvastatin and rosuvastatin are the only statins that reach high intensity (50% or greater lowering of LDL, low-density lipoprotein, cholesterol). — Lipids.pptx, Slide 68"},{"q":"Which illnesses typically precede Reye's syndrome?","choices":["Bacterial otitis media or sinusitis","Streptococcal pharyngitis","Gastroenteritis from food poisoning","An upper respiratory infection"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. All viral, which is why the aspirin restriction in children is framed around fever from viral illness specifically. Influenza or chickenpox. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"Bacitracin ointment works on a structure none of the other ophthalmic antibiotics target. Which one?","choices":["The 50S ribosomal subunit","The bacterial cell wall","DNA gyrase","The cytoplasmic membrane"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Bacitracin stops mucopeptides being transferred into the growing wall. Every other ophthalmic antibiotic goes after the ribosome, DNA, folate or the membrane. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"A patient on lisinopril begins taking ibuprofen daily for knee pain. How do NSAIDs (nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drugs) affect the ACE inhibitor?","choices":["They increase its effect","They reduce its effect","They have no interaction","They cause its cough"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. NSAIDs block the prostaglandin-dependent, bradykinin-mediated relaxation that contributes to the ACE (angiotensin-converting enzyme) inhibitor's effect, so blood pressure control weakens. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"A 74-year-old on timolol drops for glaucoma develops systemic side effects. By what route did the drug reach the circulation?","choices":["Nasolacrimal drainage","Transcorneal absorption into the bloodstream","Absorption through the eyelid skin","Swallowed residue from the eyelashes"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. What runs down the duct is absorbed systemically without passing through the liver first. Which also avoids first-pass metabolism. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"Which statement about ACE (angiotensin-converting enzyme) inhibitors in pregnancy is accurate?","choices":["Contraindicated in the second and third trimesters","Safe once the first trimester has ended","Contraindicated in the first trimester only","Preferred for hypertension throughout pregnancy"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. They cause fetal morbidity and mortality, including birth defects and fetal death, so they are contraindicated in the second and third trimesters. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"When resins deplete the bile acid pool, which hepatic enzyme increases to convert cholesterol into bile acids?","choices":["Acyl-CoA:cholesterol acyltransferase","Lipoprotein lipase in plasma","Cholesterol 7-alpha hydroxylase","Hepatic HMG-CoA reductase"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Increased 7-alpha hydroxylase activity converts more cholesterol to bile acids, and the liver adds LDL receptors, increasing VLDL and LDL removal. — Lipids.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"Which cells are the principal targets in ocular hypersensitivity, and what happens when immunoglobulin E binds them?","choices":["Neutrophils and macrophages; binding triggers phagocytosis","T lymphocytes; binding triggers interleukin-2 release","Corneal epithelial cells; binding triggers prostaglandin release","Mast cells and basophils, within seconds"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Immunoglobulin E binds Fc receptors, tyrosine kinases activate within 5 to 15 seconds, and histamine, platelet-activating factor and leukotrienes are released — producing vasodilation, swelling, redness and itch. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"A patient on diltiazem is started on amiodarone. What pharmacodynamic risk does the combination carry?","choices":["Excess slowing of the heart","Reflex tachycardia","Hyperkalemia","Loss of diltiazem effect"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Amiodarone with a non-dihydropyridine can slow the sinus rate or worsen atrioventricular block; digoxin and beta blockers are listed alongside it. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"Which risk factor for coronary heart disease can a patient change?","choices":["Age older than 45 in men","Age older than 55 in women","Tobacco use","Family history of premature events"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Tobacco use, a sedentary lifestyle, diabetes, overweight, hypertension and dyslipidemia are modifiable risk factors that therapy targets. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"What distinguishes angle closure glaucoma from open angle glaucoma?","choices":["Increased aqueous production with a gradual course","Blockage of the drainage canal, with acute pain","Optic nerve damage at a normal intraocular pressure","Raised pressure without any optic nerve damage"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Open angle comes from increased production or reduced drainage and damages the nerve over time; angle closure is a mechanical blockage that presents acutely and painfully. Drug therapy targets open angle disease. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 61"},{"q":"Which two classes both lower triglycerides by 20 to 50% and raise HDL (high-density lipoprotein) substantially?","choices":["Resins and ezetimibe","Fibrates and niacin","Statins and resins","Ezetimibe and statins"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Both lower triglycerides by 20 to 50% and raise HDL (niacin 15 to 35%, fibrates 10 to 35%). — Lipids.pptx, Slide 61"},{"q":"A 64-year-old with chronic kidney disease who uses a salt substitute is started on lisinopril. Which laboratory abnormality is most important to anticipate?","choices":["Hypokalemia","Hypercalcemia","Hypoglycemia","Hyperkalemia"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Hyperkalemia is most often seen with renal disease and in those taking potassium-sparing diuretics, potassium supplements or salt substitutes; this patient has two of those. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"Where in the gut do resins block the reabsorption of bile acids?","choices":["The stomach","The brush border of the jejunum","The gallbladder wall","The terminal ileum"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Bile acids are normally reabsorbed at the terminal ileum and recirculated to the liver; resins block that reabsorption so bile acid excretion rises. — Lipids.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"Why are arterial vessels 3 to 10 times more sensitive to calcium channel blockers than myocardial cells?","choices":["Myocytes lack L-type calcium channels","Arteries store more internal calcium","Arteries rely on outside calcium entry","Arteries carry more beta-2 receptors"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Arterial smooth muscle depends on exogenous calcium through L-type channels, whereas myocytes also draw on a recirculating store in the sarcoplasmic reticulum. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"How does minoxidil relax arteriolar smooth muscle?","choices":["Raises nitric oxide and cyclic GMP","Opens ATP-modulated potassium channels","Blocks L-type calcium channels","Blocks vascular alpha-1 receptors"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Increased potassium efflux hyperpolarizes the cell and relaxes the muscle; its action on potassium channels also causes arrhythmias. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 103"},{"q":"A child with acute otitis media finished a course of antibiotics three weeks ago. How does that change the choice?","choices":["The same high-dose amoxicillin is used","A macrolide is substituted","Amoxicillin-clavulanate is used instead","Antibiotics are withheld for a further week"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Recent antibiotic exposure selects for beta-lactamase producers, and that is exactly what the clavulanate is there to cover. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"Which laboratory change occurs when ezetimibe is combined with a statin?","choices":["Raised hepatic transaminases","Raised serum potassium","Raised serum uric acid","Raised serum calcium"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Ezetimibe's adverse effects are gastrointestinal effects and elevated hepatic transaminases when combined with statins. — Lipids.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"Which lipid-lowering class can RAISE triglycerides?","choices":["Fibric acid derivatives","Bile acid sequestrants","Nicotinic acid (niacin)","HMG-CoA reductase inhibitors"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Resins show no change or a rise in triglycerides, because they may increase VLDL (very low-density lipoprotein) production. — Lipids.pptx, Slide 61"},{"q":"A 52-year-old man has sudden tearing chest pain radiating to the back and ST-segment elevation. Blood pressure differs between the two arms, and imaging shows an aortic dissection. Which drug is contraindicated?","choices":["Metoprolol","Labetalol","Morphine","Tenecteplase"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Aortic dissection is a contraindication to fibrinolysis, because clot lysis can lead to catastrophic bleeding. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 63"},{"q":"A patient on a first generation antihistamine reports dry mouth, blurred vision and difficulty passing urine. What explains this?","choices":["The antimuscarinic action of the drug","Histamine receptor blockade itself","An allergic reaction to the drug","Interaction with alcohol"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Three complaints from three different organs, all explained by one receptor family being blocked. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"Which lipoprotein carries dietary fat away from the intestine?","choices":["Chylomicrons","Very low-density lipoprotein","Low-density lipoprotein","High-density lipoprotein"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Chylomicrons are secreted by intestinal cells; lipoprotein lipase hydrolyzes their triglyceride core, and the liver removes the remnants. — Lipids.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"A 72-year-old starting verapamil asks which change in bowel habit to watch for. What should she be told?","choices":["Constipation","Black stools","Oily, fatty stools","Bloody diarrhea"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Constipation is among the listed gastrointestinal effects of non-dihydropyridines, so bowel habits are worth asking about at follow-up. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 45"},{"q":"A patient's medication list includes ramipril. To which drug class does it belong?","choices":["Angiotensin receptor blockers","Dihydropyridine blockers","ACE inhibitors","Beta blockers"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The -pril suffix marks an ACE (angiotensin-converting enzyme) inhibitor; ramipril sits alongside lisinopril, enalapril, benazepril and quinapril. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"What is N-acetylcysteine traditionally used for, apart from its mucolytic action?","choices":["Acetaminophen poisoning","Aspirin poisoning","Opioid overdose","Organophosphate poisoning"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The same drug turns up in two entirely unrelated places -- as an antidote in poisoning and as a mucolytic in the airway -- which is worth noticing rather than being surprised by. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 63"},{"q":"What should a patient who drinks alcohol regularly be told about acetaminophen?","choices":["The combination risks liver damage over time","Alcohol prevents acetaminophen from working","The combination causes gastric ulceration","There is no interaction between the two drugs"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Neither is dangerous on its own at ordinary use, which is what makes the combination easy to arrive at without noticing. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"Which patients should avoid ibuprofen?","choices":["Those with a history of Reye's syndrome","Those taking acetaminophen","Those over sixty-five years old","Those with asthma it may worsen"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Asthma is the one most easily forgotten, because it is not an obvious consequence of blocking prostaglandin synthesis. Previous ulcer or perforation, or renal impairment. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"Which complications of persistent coughing are described as common?","choices":["Stroke, syncope and rib fracture","Pneumothorax, hemoptysis and chest pain","Insomnia, exhaustion, pain and hoarseness","Hearing loss, tinnitus and vertigo"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Unglamorous but genuinely disabling, and together they are the reason cough is treated at all. The pain is musculoskeletal, from the effort of coughing. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 56"},{"q":"What is the stated goal of treating infectious conjunctivitis?","choices":["Relieve symptoms while the infection resolves on its own","Prevent transmission to the other eye","Eradicate the infection and prevent long-term complications","Establish the organism by culture"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Both halves matter — clearing the organism is not the whole aim, because the reason to treat promptly is what an untreated infection leaves behind. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"Why are nitrates used in acute coronary syndrome?","choices":["To relieve chest pain","To lower mortality","To limit infarct size","To prevent reinfarction"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Nitrates give relief of chest pain only; they show no mortality benefit and no improvement in outcomes. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"A 35-year-old with predictable seasonal allergy cannot tolerate antihistamine drops and is willing to start weeks before the season. Which class suits her?","choices":["An ophthalmic antibiotic","A vasoconstrictor used daily through the season","A mast cell stabilizer","A topical glucocorticoid through the season"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Slow to work — 5 to 14 days — but that matters less when treatment starts before the season. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"Which drug manages chest pain that does not respond to nitrates in ST-elevation myocardial infarction?","choices":["Metoprolol","Aspirin","Alteplase","Morphine"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Morphine, an opioid analgesic, is used for chest pain unresponsive to nitrates in ST-elevation myocardial infarction. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 56"},{"q":"Which effect on blood lipids has been reported with prazosin?","choices":["A rise in triglycerides","A rise in LDL","A mild rise in HDL","A large fall in HDL"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Prazosin shows evidence of a mild reduction in LDL (low-density lipoprotein) and triglycerides and increased HDL (high-density lipoprotein). — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 78"},{"q":"How is acetaminophen tolerated when taken as intended?","choices":["Poorly, with frequent gastric irritation","Very well, with no notable side effects","With common renal impairment","With frequent sedation"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Its safety within the intended range is exactly why exceeding that range is so easy to do. That holds at therapeutic exposure, which is what makes exceeding it so easy. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"H1 and H2 receptors both cause vasodilation. How do the two differ?","choices":["H1 is slow and sustained, H2 is rapid and brief","H1 is rapid in onset and short-lived","Both are rapid and brief","Only H1 causes vasodilation"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Same end result by two different routes and on two different timescales, which is why blocking H1 alone does not abolish the flush. H2 is slow in onset and long-lasting. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"What are the main uses of ocular glucocorticoids?","choices":["First-line treatment of all allergic conjunctivitis","Bacterial keratitis, to limit scarring","Chronic dry eye as maintenance therapy","Severe allergy, uveitis and post-surgical inflammation"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. They are generally reserved for refractory symptoms, and can be given topically or intraocularly depending on the indication. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"What is the primary indication for a fibrate?","choices":["LDL over 190 in a young adult","High LDL during pregnancy","Statin-induced raised enzymes","Triglycerides over 1000 or low HDL"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The primary indication is triglycerides above 1000 mg/dL or low HDL (high-density lipoprotein) cholesterol. — Lipids.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"A patient with high triglycerides has a history of symptomatic gout. Which triglyceride-lowering option is best avoided?","choices":["Fenofibrate","Gemfibrozil","Niacin","Bezafibrate"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Niacin raises uric acid, so symptomatic gout and significant hyperuricemia are relative contraindications. — Lipids.pptx, Slide 56"}]};
